  "deletedBy": null,
  "details": "Floating point operations (example 2.4f * 3.8f) are currently very slow in Udon to the point that many scripts take vastly more CPU time per frame than necessary, which artificially makes most of VRChat's most compelling, interactive content needlessly inaccessible to casual and mobile users. This could be very easily solved by simply adding an SDK component which can be added to GameObjects in editor which can bake a formula into an n-dimensional look-up table, that can be accessed by Udon at runtime. \n\nThis component should bake into a lookup table (like a texture but in RAM) where the world dev can specify the number of inputs (dimensions) and a formula (shape) and keys (points) in editor. At runtime, an Udon script would then read/write to the input and read from the output. \n\nEven though this might call one extern or require two frames to get a result, it would still allow us to do astronomically more math in the space of two frames than could ever be remotely possible currently, and without adding any meaningful CPU time. If anything, it makes it possible to remove costly organization and control operations from Udon scripts, which will decrease CPU time substantially.\n\nMany scripts currently have to break up complex math over hundreds of frames in order to limit Udon's (extremely time-expensive) \"externs\" per frame, this component would allow many complex systems such as vehicle systems and games to use far less code, run far fewer control operations (if, switch) which they currently use just to spread things out. It would mean huge performance gains without changing anything about the Udon or Soba VM and allow more interesting and unique experiences to become available to lower-end hardware than is currently possible.",
